 ("p", "A huge place can fit on a small sheet of paper because of the map's **scale**. If the scale is 1 cm = 500 m, then every centimetre on the map stands for 500 metres on the ground. On the map of India in the book, a ruler 2.5 cm long stands for 500 km. So the real distance between two places depends on the scale of the map."),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "Real distance = map distance × scale value. Example: scale 1 cm = 2 km, map distance 7.5 cm → real distance = 7.5 × 2 = 15 km."),
 ("steps", [("Example 1 — Playground map", "A playground is 40 m long and 30 m wide. On a scale of 1 cm = 10 m, the drawing is 4 cm × 3 cm. Its diagonal measures 5 cm on the paper, so the real diagonal is 5 × 10 = 50 m.")]),
 ("h3", "Direction"),
 ("p", "Most maps have an arrow marked **N** that points to the north. The four **cardinal directions** (cardinal points) are north (at the top), east, south and west, going clockwise. The **intermediate directions** lie between them: northeast (NE), southeast (SE), southwest (SW) and northwest (NW). Opposite directions pair up: N–S, E–W, NE–SW, NW–SE."),
 ("h3", "Symbols"),
 ("p", "On a large map there is no space to draw buildings and roads as they look. A **symbol** is a small standard picture that stands for a feature such as a railway station, a school, a post office, a road, a river, a pond or a forest. Different countries use different sets of symbols. The **Survey of India**, a government body, has fixed the set of symbols for maps of India. Colours work like symbols too (blue for water)."),
