 ("p", "The Greenwich Meridian is not the first prime meridian. Many centuries before Europe, India had one called **madhya rekhā** ('middle line'), which passed through **Ujjayinī** (today's Ujjain), an old centre of astronomy. The astronomer **Varāhamihira** lived and worked there about 1,500 years ago. The Ujjayinī meridian (about 75.8°E) was the reference for calculations in Indian astronomical texts. Cities such as Kurukṣhetra, Māhiṣhmati (Maheshwar), Rohitaka (Rohtak) and Kumārī (Kanyakumari) are mentioned as being on or near it. Some are a little away from the line because measuring longitude needs accurate timekeeping, which was not as exact in those days."),
